%[text] # solve2PortNetwork
%[text] Solve the two-port network of the loudspeaker system for the diaphragm volume velocity and the source current
%[text] Class: lspsys
%%
%[text] ## Syntax
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] val = solve2PortNetwork(obj)
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] `val = solve2PortNetwork(obj)` reduces the 4-ports `tae` (enclosure) and `tarad` (radiation) of the enclosure of the `lspsys` object `obj` to the two-port network of the acoustical side, multiplies it with the transmission matrices of the driver for each frequency in `Frequency`, and solves the network for the diaphragm volume velocity, the radiated volume velocity and the source current. `createResult` calls this method; use `createResult` to get the full `result` object.
%%
%[text] ## Output Arguments
%[text] `val` — Solution of the network, returned as a structure with these fields:
%[text:table]
%[text] | Field | Symbol | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `DiaphragmVolumeVelocity` | $ U\_d $ | Volume velocity of the diaphragm in m3/s, row vector over `Frequency` |
%[text] | `RadiatedVolumeVelocity` | $ U\_{rad} $ | Volume velocity that the radiation leads to the ambient pressure in m3/s, row vector over `Frequency` |
%[text] | `SourceCurrent` | $ i\_g $ | Current of the source in A, row vector over `Frequency` |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Algorithms
%[text] The loudspeaker system is modelled as an equivalent circuit of the electrical, mechanical and acoustical domain. The symbols are listed in [Symbols](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Transmission matrices
%[text] Every block of the circuit is a two-port network, described by its transmission matrix (ABCD matrix). The matrix relates the effort $ y $ (voltage, force or pressure) and the flow $ x $ (current, velocity or volume velocity) at port 1 to those at port 2. The effort is the quantity across the two terminals of a port; the flow enters the network at port 1 and leaves it at port 2, in the direction of the arrows:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:5b1e)
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{y\_1 \\cr x\_1} \\right\] = \\left\[ \\matrix{A & B \\cr C & D} \\right\] \\left\[ \\matrix{y\_2 \\cr x\_2} \\right\] $
%[text] Two-port networks in cascade multiply: the matrix of the chain is the product of the matrices of its blocks, from the source to the load.
%%
%[text] ### Equivalent circuit
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:4a7f)
%[text] The electrical and mechanical side are a chain of two-port networks: the electrical impedance $ \\mathbf{T}\_e $, the gyrator $ \\mathbf{T}\_{bl} $, the mechanical impedance $ \\mathbf{T}\_m $ and the transformer $ \\mathbf{T}\_{sd} $ from force and velocity to pressure and volume velocity. The acoustical side is the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $, which holds 4-ports on three conductors: the front of the diaphragm (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear of the diaphragm (bottom). Port 2 of $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ stays open: inside $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $, all flow goes to the ambient pressure.
%%
%[text] ### Electrical and mechanical side
%[text] Each block relates the quantities on its left to those on its right:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_e \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_2 \\cr i\_2} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_2 \\cr i\_2} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_{bl} \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_1 \\cr u\_1} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_1 \\cr u\_1} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_m \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_2 \\cr u\_2} \\right\], \\quad \\left\[ \\matrix{f\_2 \\cr u\_2} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_{sd} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] $
%[text] so that
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_e \\mathbf{T}\_{bl} \\mathbf{T}\_m \\mathbf{T}\_{sd} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] $
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical side
%[text] $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ is a two-port network like the other blocks. Port 1 is the front and the rear conductor: $ \\mathbf{T}\_{sd} $ floats between them, so its pressure is the pressure difference over the diaphragm, $ p\_1 = p\_{f1} - p\_{r1} $. The volume velocity $ U\_d $ leaves through the front conductor and returns through the rear conductor. Inside $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $, the 4-ports lead all flow to the ambient pressure: the reference symbols. Port 2 is the front and the rear conductor at the output, with the pressure difference $ p\_2 $ and the flow $ U\_2 $; it stays open, so $ U\_2 = 0 $. The flows are counted in the direction of the arrows: to the right on the front conductor and to the left on the rear conductor.
%[text] Inside $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $, the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation lie in cascade. Together they can model any linear enclosure and radiation, for example a closed box, a bass reflex or a horn, and radiation from the diaphragm, a port or a mouth:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:8c3d)
%[text] Each 4-port has a 4 × 4 transmission matrix. Its 2 × 2 blocks $ \\mathbf{T}\_{ff} $ and $ \\mathbf{T}\_{rr} $ describe what the 4-port does at the front and at the rear of the diaphragm, including elements to the ambient pressure; $ \\mathbf{T}\_{fr} $ and $ \\mathbf{T}\_{rf} $ describe coupling between front and rear, for example a leak or a vent, and are zero when the sides are independent:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{fk} \\cr U\_{fk} \\cr p\_{rk} \\cr U\_{rk}} \\right\] = \\left\[ \\matrix{T\_{ff} & \\mathbf{T}\_{fr} \\cr \\mathbf{T}\_{rf} & \\mathbf{T}\_{rr}} \\right\] \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f,k+1} \\cr U\_{f,k+1} \\cr p\_{r,k+1} \\cr U\_{r,k+1}} \\right\] $
%[text] The two 4-ports multiply to one 4 × 4 matrix $ \\mathbf{M} = \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $, with the rows $ \\mathbf{m}\_1 $ to $ \\mathbf{m}\_4 $. At the input, both flows are $ U\_d $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f1} \\cr U\_d \\cr p\_{r1} \\cr U\_d} \\right\] = \\mathbf{M} \\mathbf{x}\_3, \\quad \\mathbf{x}\_3 = \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_{f3} \\cr U\_{f3} \\cr p\_{r3} \\cr U\_{r3}} \\right\] $
%%
%[text] ### Transmission matrix of the acoustical side
%[text] At port 2, the pressure difference is $ p\_2 = p\_{f3} - p\_{r3} $ and both conductors carry the flow of the port, $ U\_{f3} = U\_{r3} = U\_2 $. The pressure $ p\_{r3} $ of the rear conductor against the reference is not fixed by the port. With $ \\mathbf{w} = \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr U\_2} \\right\] $ this is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathbf{x}\_3 = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{q} p\_{r3}, \\quad \\mathbf{P} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1 \\cr 0 & 0 \\cr 0 & 1} \\right\], \\quad \\mathbf{q} = \\left\[ \\matrix{1 \\cr 0 \\cr 1 \\cr 0} \\right\] $
%[text] Rows 2 and 4 of $ \\mathbf{M} $ both give $ U\_d $, so their difference is zero. That fixes $ p\_{r3} $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p\_{r3} = -\\frac{(\\mathbf{m}\_2 - \\mathbf{m}\_4) \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{w}}{(\\mathbf{m}\_2 - \\mathbf{m}\_4) \\mathbf{q}}, \\quad \\mathbf{x}\_3 = \\mathbf{K} \\mathbf{w}, \\quad \\mathbf{K} = \\mathbf{P} - \\frac{\\mathbf{q} (\\mathbf{m}\_2 - \\mathbf{m}\_4) \\mathbf{P}}{(\\mathbf{m}\_2 - \\mathbf{m}\_4) \\mathbf{q}} $
%[text] The difference of rows 1 and 3 gives $ p\_1 $ and row 2 gives $ U\_d $, which is the 2 × 2 transmission matrix of the acoustical side:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_1 \\cr U\_d} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T}\_a \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr U\_2} \\right\], \\quad \\mathbf{T}\_a = \\left\[ \\matrix{\\mathbf{m}\_1 - \\mathbf{m}\_3 \\cr \\mathbf{m}\_2} \\right\] \\mathbf{K} $
%%
%[text] ### Solution
%[text] All blocks are two-port networks, so the chain is one matrix product. Port 2 of $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ is open, $ U\_2 = 0 $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\left\[ \\matrix{e\_g \\cr i\_g} \\right\] = \\mathbf{T} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr 0} \\right\], \\quad \\mathbf{T} = \\mathbf{T}\_e \\mathbf{T}\_{bl} \\mathbf{T}\_m \\mathbf{T}\_{sd} \\mathbf{T}\_a $
%[text] The first row of the chain gives $ p\_2 $ and the second row the source current; the diaphragm volume velocity follows from $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $. For each frequency:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p\_2 = \\frac{e\_g}{T\_{11}}, \\quad i\_g = T\_{21} p\_2, \\quad U\_d = T\_{a,21} p\_2 $
%[text] The quantities at the output of the radiation 4-port follow from $ \\mathbf{x}\_3 = \\mathbf{K} \\left\[ \\matrix{p\_2 \\cr 0} \\right\] $, those at its input from $ \\mathbf{x}\_2 = \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} \\mathbf{x}\_3 $. The flow that the radiation 4-port leads to the ambient pressure is the radiated volume velocity; the rear flows count to the left, so they enter with a minus sign:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ U\_{rad} = (U\_{f2} - U\_{f3}) - (U\_{r2} - U\_{r3}) $
%[text] `createResult` uses $ U\_{rad} $ for the sound pressure. For a closed box, all volume velocity of the diaphragm flows through the radiation impedance at the front, so $ U\_{rad} = U\_d $.
%%
